'''Tōru Funamura''' (船村 徹, {{Birth date and age|1932|6|12|no}} – {{Death date and age|2017|2|16}})<ref name="Mainichi">{{cite web |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210618173803/http://mainichi.jp/articles/20170217/k00/00e/040/191000c |title=Obituary: Tōru Funamura (84 years old), composer, recipient of the Order of Culture |publisher=[[Mainichi Shimbun]] |date=2017-02-17 |accessdate=2023-10-27 }}</ref> was a Japanese {{ill|Composer|en|作曲家}} and {{ill|Singer|en|歌手}}. He served as the honorary chairman of the {{ill|Japanese Society for Rights of Authors, Composers and Publishers (JASRAC)|en|日本音楽著作権協会}}, and as the chief advisor of the {{ill|Japanese Composer's Association|en|日本作曲家協会}}. He was also a member of the {{ill|Yokozuna Deliberation Council|en|横綱審議委員会}}. His real name was Hiroo Fukuda (福田 博郎). A leading composer in Japan’s post-war popular music scene, Funamura composed over 5,000 songs and was the first composer of Japanese pop music to receive the {{ill|Order of Culture|en|文化勲章}}. His court rank was {{ill|Junior Third Rank|en|従三位}}.

