'''Tōru Funamura''' (船村 徹, {{Birth date and age|1932|6|12|no}} – {{Death date and age|2017|2|16}})<ref name="Mainichi">{{cite web |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210618173803/http://mainichi.jp/articles/20170217/k00/00e/040/191000c |title=Obituary: Tōru Funamura (84 years old), composer, recipient of the Order of Culture |publisher=[[Mainichi Shimbun]] |date=2017-02-17 |accessdate=2023-10-27 }}</ref> was a Japanese {{ill|Composer|ja|作曲家|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Composer|qq=}} and {{ill|Singer|ja|歌手|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Singer|qq=}}. He served as the honorary chairman of the {{ill|Japanese Society for Rights of Authors, Composers and Publishers (JASRAC)|ja|日本音楽著作権協会|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Japanese Society for Rights of Authors, Composers and Publishers (JASRAC)|qq=}}, and as the chief advisor of the {{ill|Japanese Composer's Association|ja|日本作曲家協会|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Japanese Composer's Association|qq=}}. He was also a member of the {{ill|Yokozuna Deliberation Council|ja|横綱審議委員会|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yokozuna Deliberation Council|qq=}}. His real name was Hiroo Fukuda (福田 博郎). A leading composer in Japan’s post-war popular music scene, Funamura composed over 5,000 songs and was the first composer of Japanese pop music to receive the {{ill|Order of Culture|ja|文化勲章|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Order of Culture|qq=}}. His court Rank was {{ill|Junior Third Rank|ja|従三位|1=Junior Third Rank|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Junior Third Rank|13=User:Immanuelle/Junior Third Rank|qq=}}.

[[File:船村徹記念館1.jpg|thumb|right|Tōru Funamura Memorial Hall]]

